# Resolution
Fixed by [`afea45b4`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/afea45b4)
(`Enforce route admission across streaming writes`).

Streaming PUT, POST Object, and UploadPart body waits are now bounded by both
the configured per-frame idle timeout and the immutable process-monotonic
deadline captured by their route admission. A same-generation lease renewal
cannot extend that request deadline. Expiry returns `OperationAborted`, stops
the request from performing further storage effects, and releases the route
admission instead of allowing a trickle upload to hold publication in
`Draining` indefinitely.

Every frontend-created stream session persists the admission's authority-clock
deadline as a durable cleanup handoff. The request derives a non-cloneable,
object-scoped retained cleanup capability before its first durable stream
mutation and makes one prompt abort attempt on expiry. If that attempt cannot
reach the old acting set, the frontend still releases admission; the
independent stream-session sweeper follows the current runtime map and removes
the expired session, reservation, acknowledgements, and staged shards. PUT and
POST heartbeat tasks retain only weak context references, so a sleeping
heartbeat cannot extend admission lifetime after the request has unwound.

Admission is also enforced at each storage-effect boundary. Initial PUT
authorization/reservation preparation, POST session creation, UploadPart
session creation, append, direct commit, heartbeat, and finalization all
revalidate the captured admission against the exact runtime-map generation
used for the operation. Tests renew the raw same-epoch cluster beyond the old
deadline and prove that the raw cluster remains admissible while the earlier
request is rejected before creating a reservation or session. This closes the
renewal-based bypass as well as the original slow-body path.

Runtime-map installation now revalidates a bounded candidate after admitted
requests drain and immediately before publication. A candidate that expires
during the drain is rejected without replacing the current map, and the gate
returns to `Open` for later requests.

Deterministic regressions cover publication blocking and release, post-drain
candidate expiry, promoted PUT/POST/UploadPart cleanup, failed prompt cleanup
with later durable sweeping, same-store runtime-map refresh, sleeping
heartbeats, same-epoch renewal at initial and final storage effects, and
cross-generation admission misuse. Formatting, the storage boundary checker,
and workspace strict Clippy passed. The preceding complete workspace run
passed 7,363 tests; the final initial-mutation regression brought the suite to
7,364 tests, whose rerun reached a separately fixed conditional-delete race
before nextest cancelled the remainder.

# Summary
Introduced: per-request route admission is retained across long streaming request bodies and globally blocks runtime-map publication and later admissions during publication. This creates a network-triggerable availability issue for control-plane-enabled deployments by allowing a slow authenticated streaming upload to stall frontend route updates and storage request handling.
The new admission gate increments active_requests for each admitted route and route publication switches the gate to Draining, then waits until active_requests reaches zero. Streaming PUT/POST/UploadPart now stores the admission guard inside the streaming context, and the async body loop keeps that context alive while waiting for each body frame. Because the body timeout is per-frame, a client that is allowed to start a streaming write can keep the request alive by sending data slowly. When the control-plane refresh loop tries to install a new runtime map, begin_publication blocks until that slow request ends, and all new admissions block behind the Draining state. Additionally, install only checks that the candidate has a bounded validity before waiting; it does not re-check that the candidate is still valid after the potentially long drain, so a delayed publication can install an already-expired map and cause subsequent admissions to fail until another refresh succeeds.

