# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Kept at medium. Static evidence and the prior executable PoC support that the bug is real and in main runtime code: streaming contexts retain route admission while body loops wait per frame, and install/begin_publication globally drain active requests. The attack is reachable from the S3 HTTP data plane for write-authorized clients and can affect other clients, so it is security-relevant availability impact. It does not justify high/critical because confidentiality and integrity are not directly compromised, exploitation requires write authorization or public-write configuration plus control-plane-enabled runtime-map refresh, default exposure is localhost:9000 with legacy-local role, and existing max connection/in-flight limits bound concurrency even though they do not prevent the single-request stall.
## Likelihood
high - The exploit mechanics are low complexity after prerequisites: start a write-authorized streaming upload and trickle frames under the per-frame timeout. Likelihood is moderated by requiring a reachable listener, write permission or public-write policy, and a non-default control-plane/runtime-map refresh deployment. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - A single slow admitted streaming write can stall runtime-map publication and block later storage-route admissions during Draining; validation also showed an expired candidate can be published after a delayed drain. This is service availability impact, not direct data theft, privilege escalation, or code execution.
## Assumptions
- The deployment uses the control-plane/runtime-map refresh path, e.g. a frontend or combined role with ARGMIN_CONTROL_PLANE_SOCKET_PATH configured.
- The attacker can reach the argmin-s3 HTTP listener and can start a write operation, either with valid S3 write credentials or via an intentionally public-write bucket/policy.
- A control-plane route-map publication/refresh occurs while the attacker's streaming upload is still admitted.
- reachable HTTP S3 endpoint
- write-authorized PutObject, POST Object, or UploadPart request
- control-plane-enabled runtime-map refresh/publication path
## Path
S3 client -> streaming write -> admission permit held during slow body -> runtime-map install -> Draining waits for active_requests=0 -> later requests stall/fail
## Path evidence
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:440-495` - Default listener is 127.0.0.1:9000 and max connection/in-flight request limits are environment-configurable.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:6548-6632` - The main argmin-s3 binary binds a TCP listener, optionally enables TLS, and starts the HTTP server with configured connection and in-flight limits.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:6635-6657` - If a control-plane socket is configured, the frontend starts the runtime-map refresh loop that can call the blocking publication path.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:4142-4145` - Streaming PutObject authenticates and then acquires storage route admission before body streaming.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:4274-4277` - The acquired route admission is stored in StreamingPutContext, extending the permit lifetime.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:4777-4820` - Streaming PutObject, PostObject, and UploadPart contexts all contain _storage_route_admission.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:182-213` - ServeConfig documents body_idle_timeout as a per-frame idle timeout that resets on each chunk and defaults to 30 seconds.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:3112-3125` - The PutObject streaming loop keeps the Arc context alive while waiting for each frame with tokio::time::timeout(idle_timeout, body.frame()).
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:3996-4001` - UploadPart uses the same per-frame timeout body loop while the streaming part context is live.
- `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:1302-1321` - Route admission waits unless the transition state is Open and increments active_requests for admitted requests.
- `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:1324-1347` - Runtime-map publication sets the shared gate to Draining and waits until active_requests reaches zero.
- `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:1387-1399` - The active request count is decremented only when the admission permit is dropped.
- `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:1610-1624` - install checks only that the candidate has bounded validity before begin_publication, with no post-drain validity recheck at this point.
- `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:1767-1787` - Control-plane runtime-map refresh obtains a candidate and calls install, reaching the blocking publication path.
## Narrative
The finding is a real, reachable availability issue for control-plane-enabled argmin-s3 deployments. Streaming write handlers authenticate/authorize, then acquire a storage route admission and store it in the streaming context. The async body loops keep that context alive while waiting on a per-frame idle timeout, so a slow client can keep active_requests nonzero with minimal traffic. Runtime-map install calls begin_publication, which changes the shared gate to Draining and waits for all active requests to finish; while not Open, later admissions wait. The code also only checks that a candidate has bounded validity before the drain, not that it is still valid after a prolonged wait. Severity remains medium: it is network-triggerable and can affect service availability, but it requires write authorization or public-write configuration plus a control-plane refresh deployment, and it does not directly compromise confidentiality or integrity.
## Controls
- S3 SigV4/header or POST policy authentication before ordinary private writes
- Coordinator authorization for PutObject/PostObject/UploadPart writes
- Configurable max_connections and max_inflight_requests
- Header and per-frame body idle timeouts
- Optional TLS via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH
- Non-root process startup enforcement
## Blindspots
- No Kubernetes, Terraform, Docker Compose, or other deployment manifests were present, so actual public exposure and load balancer type could not be confirmed.
- README describes frontend/combined remote-routing modes as in-progress/unsupported, while current code has configuration and tests for control-plane frontend refresh; real-world prevalence is uncertain.
- Static repository review cannot measure operator-specific upload timeouts, reverse-proxy buffering, or external rate limiting that may reduce exploitability.
